{
    "file": "Overcoming_Sales_Objections.docx",
    "title": "Overcoming Sales Objections",
    "duration": "3 Days",
    "overview": [
        "Sales objections are a natural part of customer decision making. They may reflect genuine concerns about value, cost, timing, suitability, implementation, trust, or the risks of change. Effective sales professionals do not treat every objection as resistance to defeat; they investigate its underlying cause, respond with relevant evidence, and help customers reach well-informed decisions.",
        "This three-day course develops a structured, consultative approach to anticipating, clarifying, and resolving objections across the sales process. Participants learn to distinguish objections from conditions and brush-offs, listen without defensiveness, diagnose customer concerns, communicate value, respond to price pressure, manage competing alternatives, and confirm whether concerns have been resolved.",
        "Practical scenarios, questioning drills, role plays, case analysis, and objection-handling simulations help participants strengthen confidence while preserving trust, customer choice, and commercial value."
    ],
    "audience": [
        "Sales representatives, account executives, business development professionals, retail and field salespeople, telesales and inside-sales teams, key account managers, sales supervisors, and managers responsible for coaching customer-facing sales teams."
    ],
    "methodology": [
        "Interactive presentations, guided discussion, customer-case analysis, objection-classification exercises, questioning and listening drills, value-proposition workshops, price-objection simulations, paired role plays, peer feedback, and personal sales improvement planning."
    ],
    "competencies": [
        "Consultative objection handling",
        "Active listening and empathy",
        "Diagnostic questioning",
        "Customer-needs analysis",
        "Value communication",
        "Price and negotiation discipline",
        "Competitive differentiation",
        "Trust-building communication",
        "Handling difficult conversations",
        "Closing and commitment skills",
        "Sales resilience",
        "Self-evaluation and improvement"
    ],
    "objectivesIntro": [
        "By the end of the course, participants will be able to:"
    ],
    "objectives": [
        "Explain why objections arise and recognize their different forms.",
        "Distinguish genuine concerns from requests for clarification, conditions, and brush-offs.",
        "Apply a consistent process for acknowledging, clarifying, responding to, and checking objections.",
        "Use questions and active listening to identify the concern behind an initial statement.",
        "Respond to objections about price, value, timing, need, trust, and competitors.",
        "Support responses with customer-specific benefits and credible evidence.",
        "Manage emotional reactions and avoid defensive or high-pressure responses.",
        "Protect value and margin when discussing concessions or alternatives.",
        "Confirm resolution and agree appropriate next steps without forcing a sale.",
        "Build an objection-response library and improvement plan for their own sales environment."
    ],
